  "name": "vertiport_over_depot_v1",
  "comment": "THE HERO-SHOT BACKEND, as declared data -- now on R-7's SOURCED figures. eVTOLs land and turn around on charge pads above; ground robotaxis stage and get serviced below; delivery drones opportunity-charge at the edge -- every modality drawing on ONE 800 kW service. Airframes are real: Joby S4 (~125 kWh, GEACS ~300 kW -- Aviation Week / Joby GEACS PDF; kWh flagged +/-20%, R-7 open item), Archer Midnight (~75 kWh, 800 V, ~10-12 min turnarounds -- AIN / Archer press), Beta ALIA (~220 kWh at 115 kW CONTINUOUS -- beta.team/battery; the slow charger, and the scheduling tension). Per R-7, swap is the EXCEPTION among eVTOLs (Volocopter only, and its pack kWh is NOT FOUND) -- so the swap dock is REMOVED until fixed-duration operations exist and a swap OEM has sourced numbers; assumption (1) of the previous revision is thereby resolved by deletion, not by promotion. ready_by slack uses R-7's demonstrated-capability band (~20-40 min: 7-12 min block times per Joby/Delta, Archer/United filings + 10-12 min charge), not the earlier 65-85 min airline-style placeholder -- R-7 measured that as ~3x too generous. REMAINING LABELED INFERENCE, per R-7: pad min_gap_min=6 (no regulator publishes a time -- downwash criteria are velocity-distance; R-7 Q2 NOT FOUND); drone battery/charge (~2 kWh / ~3 kW derived from the 10-min/10-mile energy budget; no vendor publishes kWh -- R-7 Q4); generic charge-taper curves (per-OEM curves unpublished). ARRIVAL SHAPE: commuter-peak rush, because that is the demand pattern vertiports exist for. All of it is scenario DATA -- correcting any figure edits this file, never the solver.",
